Container vs Virtual Machine

Container

Shares host OS kernel
Lightweight
Starts in seconds
Common runtimes: Docker, containerd, CRI-O

Virtual Machine

Has its own guest OS
Heavier
Starts in minutes
Runs on Hypervisors like VMware, Hyper-V, KVM

"What is a container?"

A container is a lightweight, portable unit that packages an application along with its dependencies and runs in isolation while sharing the host operating system kernel. Containers provide consistency across environments and are commonly managed using Docker and orchestrated using Kubernetes

Kubernetes is a container orchestration platform that automates deployment, scaling, networking, and management of containerized applications..

What happens if each Kubernetes component goes down?
1. API Server Down
Plain Text
kubectl --> API Server ❌
Impact

✅ Existing pods keep running.

❌ Cannot create pods.

❌ Cannot delete pods.

❌ Cannot scale deployments.

❌ Controllers and Scheduler stop receiving updates.

Example
Shell
kubectl get pods

Returns:

Plain Text
Unable to connect to the server
Running Applications?

✅ Continue running because containers are already on worker nodes.

2. ETCD Down
Plain Text
API Server --> ETCD ❌
Impact

Kubernetes loses its source of truth.

Cannot:

Create Pods
Update Deployments
Scale workloads
Store cluster state
Existing Pods?

✅ Continue running.

Worst Case

If API Server restarts:

Plain Text
No ETCD
=
No cluster state

Cluster becomes unusable.

Interview Answer

ETCD failure is one of the most critical failures because it stores the entire cluster state.

3. Scheduler Down
Plain Text
Pod Created
↓
Pending
↓
Scheduler ❌
Impact

Existing pods:

✅ Run normally

New pods:

❌ Stay Pending

Example:

Shell
kubectl get pods
Plain Text
nginx Pending

Reason:

Plain Text
No scheduler available
Existing Nodes

Continue serving traffic.

4. Controller Manager Down

Controllers stop working.

Examples:

Plain Text
Deployment Controller
Node Controller
ReplicaSet Controller
Job Controller
Impact

Suppose:

YAML
replicas: 3

One pod crashes.

Normally:

Plain Text
Controller creates replacement pod

But now:

Plain Text
Pod dies
No replacement created
Effects

❌ Self-healing lost

❌ Replica maintenance lost

❌ Failed nodes not detected

✅ Existing pods continue running

5. Kubelet Down

Node Agent failure.

Plain Text
Worker Node
↓
Kubelet ❌
Impact

Node becomes:

Plain Text
NotReady

Check:

Shell
kubectl get nodes
 

Output:

Plain Text
worker-1 NotReady
Running Pods

May continue running temporarily.

But:

❌ Health status not reported

❌ New pods cannot start

❌ API Server cannot manage node

After 5 Minutes

Node Controller marks node unhealthy and starts rescheduling workloads elsewhere.

6. Container Runtime Down

Example:

Plain Text
containerd ❌

or

Plain Text
CRI-O ❌
Impact

Cannot start containers.

Cannot stop containers.

Cannot pull images.

Existing Containers

Depends on runtime crash.

Commonly:

❌ Containers eventually stop.

Node becomes unhealthy.

Check:

Shell
systemctl status containerd
7. Kube-Proxy Down
Plain Text
Service
↓
Kube-Proxy ❌
Impact
Existing Connections

May work.

New Service Traffic

May fail.

Example:

Plain Text
Frontend Service
↓
Backend Pods

Load balancing stops updating.

Pod-to-Pod Communication

✅ Usually works.

Service Access

❌ Can fail.

Cilium Interview Twist

If using:

Plain Text
Cilium kube-proxy replacement

Then kube-proxy failure doesn't matter because eBPF handles services.

8. CoreDNS Down
Plain Text
myapp.default.svc.cluster.local

DNS resolution fails.

Impact

Applications using names fail:

Shell
curl mysql-service

Fails.

Direct Pod IP
Shell
curl 10.0.1.25

May still work.

Symptoms
Plain Text
Temporary failure in name resolution
9. Worker Node Down
Plain Text
Node Crash
Impact

All pods on that node disappear.

Controller Manager notices.

Deployment recreates pods elsewhere.

Example:

Plain Text
Node1
├── PodA
├── PodB

Node1 dies.

Deployment creates:

Plain Text
Node2
├── PodA
├── PodB
10. Master Node Down (Single Master)
Plain Text
Control Plane ❌
Impact

Entire control plane unavailable.

Existing apps:

✅ Usually keep running.

Cannot:

❌ Deploy

❌ Scale

❌ Schedule

❌ Modify cluster

This is why production uses:

Plain Text
3 Control Plane Nodes

for High Availability.

Most Important Interview Summary
Component Down	What HappensAPI Server	Cluster cannot be managed
ETCD	Cluster state lost/unavailable
Scheduler	New Pods stay Pending
Controller Manager	No self-healing
Kubelet	Node becomes NotReady
Container Runtime	Containers cannot run
Kube-Proxy	Service routing issues
CoreDNS	DNS resolution fails
Worker Node	Pods rescheduled elsewhere
Control Plane	Existing apps run, cluster management stops
30-Second Senior-Level Answer

If API Server goes down, cluster management stops. If ETCD goes down, the cluster loses its source of truth. If Scheduler is down, new pods remain Pending. If Controller Manager is down, self-healing stops. If Kubelet is down, the node becomes NotReady. If Container Runtime fails, containers cannot run. If CoreDNS fails, service discovery breaks. If Kube-Proxy fails, service routing is impacted. Existing running workloads generally continue until a node or runtime failure occurs.

