Plain Text
User / DevOps Engineer
|
kubectl
|
+---------------+
| API Server |
+---------------+
| | |
| | |
etcd <---+ | +----> Scheduler
|
+----> Controller Manager
|
+----> Cloud Controller Manager
|
AWS/Azure/GCP
 
====================================================
Worker Nodes
====================================================
 
+-------------------------------+
| Node-1 |
| |
| Kubelet |
| Kube Proxy / eBPF(Cilium) |
| Container Runtime |
| (containerd) |
| |
| Pod A Pod B |
+-------------------------------+
 
+-------------------------------+
| Node-2 |
| |
| Kubelet |
| Kube Proxy / eBPF |
| Container Runtime |
| |
| Pod C |
+-------------------------------+
1. Control Plane Components

This is the brain of Kubernetes.

API Server
Plain Text
kube-apiserver

Central entry point.

Every Kubernetes component talks to API Server.

Examples:

Shell
kubectl get pods
kubectl apply -f deploy.yaml

Flow:

Plain Text
kubectl
|
API Server

Interview Question:

Can Scheduler directly talk to etcd?

Answer:

No.

All components communicate through API Server.

etcd
Plain Text
Distributed Key-Value Database

Stores cluster state.

Examples:

Plain Text
Pods
Deployments
Services
Secrets
ConfigMaps
Node Information

Think:

Plain Text
etcd = Source of Truth

Example:

Shell
kubectl create deployment nginx

Stored in etcd as desired state.

Interview Answer:

Plain Text
If API Server dies,
cluster cannot be managed.
 
If etcd dies,
cluster state is lost.
Scheduler
Plain Text
kube-scheduler
 

Responsibility:

Plain Text
Find the best node
for a pending pod.

Checks:

CPU
Memory
Taints
Affinity
Node selectors

Example:

YAML
replicas: 3

Deployment creates 3 Pods.

Scheduler chooses:

Plain Text
Pod1 -> Node1
Pod2 -> Node2
Pod3 -> Node1

Interview Question:

What happens if scheduler is down?

Answer:

Plain Text
Existing pods continue running.
New pods cannot be scheduled.
Controller Manager
Plain Text
kube-controller-manager

Runs control loops.

Examples:

Deployment Controller

Desired:

Plain Text
3 replicas

Actual:

Plain Text
2 running

Controller notices mismatch and creates 1 more pod.

Node Controller

Monitors nodes.

If node becomes unreachable:

Plain Text
Node NotReady

Pods rescheduled elsewhere.

ReplicaSet Controller

Maintains replica count.

Interview Answer:

Plain Text
Controllers continuously reconcile
actual state with desired state.
Cloud Controller Manager

Used mainly in EKS, AKS, GKE.

Integrates Kubernetes with cloud.

Examples:

Plain Text
ELB Creation
Route Tables
Volumes
Instances

Example:

YAML
type: LoadBalancer

AWS creates:

Plain Text
ALB/NLB

Automatically.

2. Worker Node Components

A node that runs applications.

Kubelet

Most important component on worker node.

Responsibilities:

Plain Text
Receive instructions from API Server
Start Pods
Monitor Pods
Send status back

Example:

Plain Text
API Server
|
Kubelet
|
containerd
|
Pod

Interview Question:

Why doesn't API Server directly start containers?

Answer:

Plain Text
API Server communicates with Kubelet.
Kubelet communicates with runtime.
Container Runtime

Examples:

Plain Text
containerd
CRI-O

Old:

Plain Text
Docker

Responsibility:

Plain Text
Pull image
Run container
Stop container

Example:

YAML
image: nginx

Runtime pulls:

Plain Text
docker.io/nginx
Kube Proxy

Provides Service networking.

Traditionally uses:

Plain Text
iptables
IPVS

Example:

Plain Text
Service IP
10.100.0.10

Packet reaches kube-proxy.

kube-proxy selects backend Pod.

Plain Text
Service
|
kube-proxy
|
Pod
Cilium (eBPF Mode)

Modern clusters replace kube-proxy.

Packet flow:

Plain Text
Client
↓
Service
↓
eBPF Program
↓
Backend Pod

No iptables traversal.

Benefits:

Plain Text
Lower latency
Better scalability
Observability
Network Policies

This is frequently asked in interviews.

Complete Pod Creation Flow

Suppose:

Shell
kubectl apply -f nginx.yaml
Step 1

kubectl sends request.

Plain Text
kubectl
|
API Server
Step 2

API Server stores object in etcd.

Plain Text
API Server
|
etcd
Step 3

Deployment Controller sees new Deployment.

Creates ReplicaSet.

Plain Text
Deployment
|
ReplicaSet
Step 4

ReplicaSet creates Pod.

Plain Text
Pod = Pending
Step 5

Scheduler selects node.

Plain Text
Pending Pod
|
Scheduler
|
Node1
Step 6

Kubelet notices assignment.

Plain Text
Node1 Kubelet

Kubelet asks containerd:

Plain Text
Pull Image
Create Container
Start Pod
Step 7

Pod becomes:

Plain Text
Running

Status goes to API Server.

Plain Text
Kubelet
|
API Server
